From 7ec49e2a0e0b7ab38dbbacb1ffb5fcc8cb24edb1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 16:01:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Late admission: decide the step after a continuing prompt
 chunk at once

Late admission (0222) decides step N+1 when step N reaches its admission
layer, so a request that arrived during N can join N+1. When N carries a
prompt chunk that is not its prompt's last, N+1's work is already fixed: that
prompt's next chunk fills N+1's budget (the long-prefill threshold leaves only
the decode rows beside it), so an arrival can at most join a later step anyway.
Deciding such a step late gains nothing and puts the decision and its dispatch
between the chunks.

On DS4.1 TP3 the Engram debug timeline of cold 32K and 131K prefills
(spark-ds41f-1022 against r6x, the same prompts) shows the next chunk's
preparation starting 44-49 ms later after each prompt's first chunk, and each
later chunk 7.7 ms slower on average; the GPU's waits for Engram rows are the
same in both (the second chunk waits 22-34 ms, later chunks not at all).

The scheduler now says whether a scheduled step continues a prompt
(SchedulerInterface.continues_prefill: a request in it still has prompt left,
is_prefill_chunk), and the engine core decides the step after such a step at
once, as async scheduling does. A step after a prompt's last chunk, or a
decode step, is still decided late. Schedulers that do not implement it keep
late admission for every step.

Base: 0222-0225 of spark-ds41f-1022 (patch head 672231a1b).

Quality: no tensor is read or written; only when the next step is decided.
Its contents cannot differ: no arrival can join a step beside a whole chunk.

Tests: tests/v1/engine/test_late_admission.py: while a prompt's first and
second chunks are in flight the next steps are decided without waiting and
nothing is admitted; an arrival during the last chunk joins the next step
(decided late); a decode step beside another prompt's continuing chunk is
decided at once.

Upstream status: local; not proposed.
